rst Goomba and let the level reload. But trace_writes needs the game running during the whole transition (it watches N frames). I can start a trace_writes for, say, 600 frames while I play_input right ~240 frames to die, then the level reloads; the trace will capture PPU palette writes. But play_input and trace_writes are separate tool calls; trace_writes runs frames itself. Actually trace_writes runs N frames (up to 600) and returns writes. It needs the game running, not paused. But play_input also runs frames. I can't do both simultaneously via separate tool calls.\n\nHmm. Options:\n- trace_writes on ppu 0x3F00 for 600 frames while the game is already doing something? The game needs to reload the level during that window. If I start trace_writes while Mario is about to die (or while the level is already transitioning), it could capture. But I can't press buttons during trace_writes.\n\nAlternative: Use a save state? Or: set up a situation where the level reloads automatically within 600 frames. E.g., Mario is standing right at the Goomba and it kills him within the trace window? But without input, Goomba may not reach.\n\nAlternative: use play_input to run into the goomba and die, then quickly call trace_writes... no, the palette load happens once during the transition; timing is tight.\n\nBetter: trace_writes on the RAM palette buffer address. But unknown.\n\nAlternative approach: Don't rely on emulator trace for the palette routine; find it by static search. Let me search PRG for code that writes the palette via $2006/$2007 in a loop. The routine likely:\n```\nlda #$3F\nsta $2006\nlda #$00\nsta $2006\nldx #$00\nloop:\n  lda PaletteData,x\n  sta $2007\n  inx\n  cpx #$20\n  bne loop\n```\nSearch for \"8D 07 20\" (STA $2007) and \"8D 06 20\". Let me search for \"8D 06 20\" occurrences and disassemble around them.\n\nActually, the routine at $8E5C might be it. Let me disassemble $8E5C (offset 0xE5C). Wait offset 0xE5C = CPU $8E5C. Let me disassemble $8E5C length 64. Actually the NMI calls $8E5C at $80E7. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The palette write through $2006/$2007 — maybe the routine that loads area palettes is `$8E5C`? Let me look at $8E5C. But $8ECD was inside a routine starting around $8E95? Let me map the routines:\n- $8E04: JumpEngine\n","truncated":true}
