t loads an area's background and sprite colors when a level starts\". So exactly two routines:\n1. Game-mode dispatch: $8212.\n2. Area palette loader: $85BF.\n\nI'll record both, and mention $8EDD as the mechanism in notes. Actually, maybe I should record the palette-write routine too as a routine mapping? The task says record each routine (the two). I could add a third mapping for the NMI VRAM/palette writer as a bonus, but the task says \"Record each with record_mapping\" referring to the two. I'll record the two required plus maybe the writer as supporting note. Actually, to be safe and useful, I'll record the two required routines with confidence 1, and also record the palette-write helper $8EDD as an additional routine (it's genuinely discovered and confirmed). Hmm, but the task says \"Call promote_mappings once at the end\" and \"Report each routine's entry\". Adding a third is fine and useful, but might dilute. I'll record the two required. Maybe also record $8EDD briefly as a note or routine. I'll include it as a third routine since it's central to the evidence and clearly proven. Actually, the instruction: \"Record each with record_mapping: mapping_type routine, id the entry address in hex...\" — \"each\" = each of the two routines. I'll record those two. I might also record the VRAM buffer writer to be thorough; the user didn't forbid it. I'll add it, clearly labeled as the hardware-write helper. Hmm, risk of \"extra\" but it's valuable. Let me include it as a routine mapping with the evidence.\n\nNow let me also double check the game-mode dispatch routine $8212 entry and evidence:\n- Entry $8212: `LDA $0770 / JSR $8E04` followed by table at $8218: [$8231, $AEDC, $838B, $9218].\n- $8E04 is JumpEngine: ASL A / TAY / PLA / STA $04 / PLA / STA $05 / INY / LDA ($04),Y / STA $06 / INY / LDA ($04),Y / STA $07 / JMP ($0006). It uses the return address (table location) and A (mode) to jump.\n- Called from NMI at $8175 (JSR $8212), gated by `LDA $0776 / LSR A / BCS