{"type":"tool_call","callId":"call_05_y4VXUy0UdoG98tfzc8J53762","tool":"record_mapping","input":{"mapping_type":"note","id":"level_start_palette_chain","name":"How a level's colours reach the PPU (World 1-1 example)","notes":"Chain proven with no reference. Level-init state machine $8567 (LDA $073C / JSR $8E04, table $856D) step 9 -> $85BF (LoadAreaPalette): LDY $074E / LDX $85BB,Y / STX $0773. Table $85BB = $01,$02,$03,$04; 1-1 has $074E=1 so $0773=2. Next NMI ($80B6-$80C3): LDX $0773 / LDA $805A,X / STA $00 / LDA $806D,X / STA $01 / JSR $8EDD. The pointer tables $805A/$806D map selector 1..4 to the four PRG palette records $8CA4,$8CC8,$8CEC,$8D10, each [3F 00 20 <32 palette bytes> 00]. $8EDD/$8E92 writes the record: STA $2006 (=$3F) / STA $2006 (=$00) then loops LDA ($00),Y / STA $2007 for the 32 bytes, setting all 4 BG + 4 sprite hardware palettes. Evidence: trace read on $85BB during a 1-1 reload = PC $85C5 reading $85BC value 2; trace read over $8CA0-$8D27 during a reload = PC $8E96/$8E9C/$8EBB/$8EE2 reading $8CC8-$8CEB; manual write $0773=1 then step_frame changed the whole palette to $8CA4 (screenshot), $0773=2 restored it. The live PPU palette for 1-1 equals the $8CC8 block (with $3F00 backdrop $22 written by the record's first byte being $0F and the engine's own backdrop write)."}}
